 "signs": [
  {
   "x": 38,
   "y": 28,
   "text": "This footbridge, opened in 1999, is named after Pero Jones, an enslaved man brought to Bristol from the Caribbean island of Nevis in 1783 by the merchant John Pinney. He lived the rest of his life in the city. The horns on the bridge are counterweights that let it lift for boats.",
   "plaque": "bristol_peros_bridge",
   "people": "black_britain",
   "title": "Pero's Bridge"
  },
  {
   "x": 50,
   "y": 28,
   "text": "In 1497 John Cabot, an Italian navigator sailing for Henry VII, left Bristol in a little ship called the Matthew and reached North America, probably Newfoundland. This is a replica built for the 500th anniversary, which sailed the Atlantic again in 1997. She still takes passengers round the harbour.",
   "plaque": "bristol_matthew",
   "title": "The Matthew"
  },
  {
   "x": 11,
   "y": 16,
   "text": "Opened in 1836, Bristol Zoo in Clifton was one of the oldest zoos in the world. Alfred the gorilla, who arrived in 1930, became the city's favourite and is still on show, stuffed, in the city museum. The zoo closed here in 2022 and moved out to a bigger site north of the city.",
   "plaque": "bristol_zoo",
   "title": "Bristol Zoo Gardens"
  },
  {
   "x": 30,
   "y": 11,
   "text": "Built in 1897 on Brandon Hill to mark 400 years since John Cabot sailed from Bristol to North America. Brandon Hill is said to be the oldest public park in Bristol, given to the city in 1174. Climb the tower's narrow stair for the best view of the harbour.",
   "plaque": "bristol_cabot_tower",
   "title": "Cabot Tower"
  },
  {
   "x": 33,
   "y": 23,
   "text": "COLLEGE GREEN. Bristol Cathedral was an abbey church founded in 1140. Its choir is a hall church, the vaults all one height, rare in Britain."
  },
  {
   "x": 45,
   "y": 26,
   "text": "A statue of Edward Colston stood here from 1895. Colston was an official of the Royal African Company, which shipped tens of thousands of enslaved Africans across the Atlantic; Bristol's ships made over 2,000 slaving voyages. In June 2020 protesters pulled the statue down and rolled it into the harbour. It is now in M Shed.",
   "plaque": "bristol_colston_plinth",
   "title": "The Empty Plinth"
  },
  {
   "x": 42,
   "y": 27,
   "text": "THE CENTRE. Once the river ran right through here; the Frome was covered over in stages, and boats used to moor where the fountains are."
  },
  {
   "x": 57,
   "y": 23,
   "text": "The four bronze pillars outside the Corn Exchange are the Nails, where merchants once counted out their money. Bristolians will tell you this is where 'paying on the nail' comes from; the phrase may be older, but the Nails are real. The Exchange clock has two minute hands: Bristol once kept its own time, about ten minutes behind London.",
   "plaque": "bristol_nails",
   "title": "The Nails"
  },
  {
   "x": 66,
   "y": 23,
   "text": "Victorian Bristol put up its warehouses in a style all its own: red brick with round arches picked out in yellow and black brick, borrowed from Moorish and Italian buildings. The Granary on Welsh Back, built in 1869, is the finest example. It later became a famous music club.",
   "plaque": "bristol_byzantine",
   "title": "Bristol Byzantine"
  },
  {
   "x": 31,
   "y": 27,
   "text": "Archibald Leach was born in Horfield, Bristol, in 1904. He ran away to join a troupe of acrobats, went to America, and became the film star Cary Grant. His bronze statue in Millennium Square, unveiled in 2001, shows him walking, as if just passing through.",
   "plaque": "bristol_cary_grant",
   "theme": "arts",
   "title": "Cary Grant"
  },
  {
   "x": 62,
   "y": 15,
   "text": "Bristol's walls became famous in the 1980s and 1990s, when local graffiti writers turned whole streets into galleries, and an anonymous Bristol artist went on to become world famous. Today murals are painted by permission here, and Upfest in Bedminster is one of Europe's biggest street art festivals.",
   "plaque": "bristol_street_art",
   "title": "Bristol's Street Art"
  },
  {
   "x": 54,
   "y": 15,
   "text": "In the 1980s sound systems like the Wild Bunch played at parties across St Pauls, mixing reggae, hip hop and soul. Out of them came Massive Attack, Portishead and Tricky, and a slow, moody sound the music press called trip-hop. St Pauls Carnival has filled these streets every summer since 1968.",
   "plaque": "bristol_trip_hop",
   "theme": "arts",
   "title": "The Bristol Sound"
  },
  {
   "x": 70,
   "y": 16,
   "text": "In 1963 the Bristol Omnibus Company refused to employ Black and Asian bus crews. Led by Roy Hackett, Paul Stephenson and others, people boycotted the buses for four months, until the company gave in on 28 August 1963. It helped bring in Britain's first laws against racial discrimination.",
   "plaque": "bristol_bus_boycott",
   "people": "windrush",
   "title": "The Bristol Bus Boycott"
  },
  {
   "x": 12,
   "y": 41,
   "text": "Designed by Isambard Kingdom Brunel and launched here in 1843, she was the first ship to bring together an iron hull and a screw propeller, and the biggest ship in the world. She carried emigrants to Australia for decades, ended up abandoned in the Falkland Islands, and was floated home to this very dock in 1970.",
   "plaque": "bristol_ss_great_britain",
   "title": "SS Great Britain"
  },
  {
   "x": 30,
   "y": 41,
   "text": "M SHED. A 1950s dockside transit shed, now Bristol's museum of Bristol. The cranes outside are the real thing and still work on special days."
  },
  {
   "x": 44,
   "y": 49,
   "text": "Britain's Concordes were built at Filton, just north of Bristol, and the first British one flew from there in 1969. Concorde crossed the Atlantic at twice the speed of sound. The very last Concorde ever to fly, Alpha Foxtrot, landed at Filton on 26 November 2003 and now lives in the Aerospace Bristol museum.",
   "plaque": "bristol_concorde",
   "title": "Concorde at Filton"
  },
  {
   "x": 49,
   "y": 43,
   "text": "Queen Elizabeth I is said to have called this 'the fairest, goodliest and most famous parish church in England'. It was built by Bristol's merchants, whose fortunes came in over the quays right below it. Its spire, broken by lightning in 1446, was only finished again in 1872.",
   "plaque": "bristol_st_mary_redcliffe",
   "title": "St Mary Redcliffe"
  },
  {
   "x": 66,
   "y": 44,
   "text": "Brunel's Great Western Railway reached here from London in 1841. He built it on a broad gauge, seven feet wide, for smooth, fast trains; the rest of the country chose narrower track, and the last broad gauge train ran in 1892. His original station, with its mock-Tudor front, still stands beside the newer one.",
   "plaque": "bristol_temple_meads",
   "title": "Temple Meads"
  },
  {
   "x": 11,
   "y": 56,
   "text": "To CLIFTON DOWN and the AVON GORGE. Mind the goats."
  }
 ],
